    "summary_one_sentence": "In 2006, investigative journalist Chris Hansen sets out to make television history with the hit sting-operation series To Catch a Predator, navigating explosive ratings and the escalating ethical fallout that follows.",
    "summary_short": "Directed by Lance Oppenheim, Primetime explores the rise and controversial fallout of the television phenomenon To Catch a Predator. In 2006, news host Chris Hansen partners with undercover online watchdogs and law enforcement to confront alleged child predators live on hidden camera. As ratings skyrocket, the line between legitimate journalism and sensationalist vigilante justice rapidly blurs. Mounting legal pressure, ethical dilemmas, and tragedy eventually threaten to dismantle the entire production.",
    "summary_long": "Set in 2006, Primetime follows television correspondent Chris Hansen as he leads NBC's To Catch a Predator, a hidden-camera investigative series aimed at exposing online child predators. Teaming up with the volunteer group Perverted-Justice and local law enforcement, Hansen sets up sting houses where suspects are confronted on television before being arrested. The show rapidly becomes a massive ratings juggernaut, turning Hansen into a household name and redefining reality crime television. However, as the production grows more ambitious to satisfy network demands, ethical boundaries begin to fray. The high-stakes environment forces the crew to navigate intense pressure, legal uncertainties, and escalating risks. Tensions culminate when a high-profile sting operation leads to a tragic suicide during a police raid, bringing fierce public and judicial scrutiny. As lawsuits mount and advertisers pull out, Hansen and his team must reckon with the real-world fallout of turning justice into prime-time spectacle. The film offers a dramatic look into the mid-2000s media landscape and the moral complexities behind true-crime entertainment.",
    "synopsis": "In the mid-2000s, broadcast journalism underwent a dramatic shift as networks sought increasingly shocking content to capture national attention. Primetime chronicles the behind-the-scenes creation and explosive surge of To Catch a Predator, the iconic news segment hosted by Chris Hansen. Working in tandem with online activist watchdogs and municipal police departments, the production orchestrated covert sting operations designed to bait and expose suspected child predators in real-time. Unfolding through high-tension confrontations inside sting houses across the country, the narrative delves into the mechanics of the show while examining how investigative reporting morphed into a sensationalized pop-culture phenomenon. As audience numbers soared, the project faced mounting questions regarding entrapment, journalistic ethics, vigilantism, and the devastating real-world consequences of turning criminal justice into public entertainment.",
